Stop treating upstream's warnings as errors in the OVMF build.

EDK II compiles with -Werror -- it is in every `gcc` line this build
runs, out of BaseTools/Conf/tools_def.template, which we do not touch.
That is upstream's discipline for upstream's own development, and it is a
good one: a warning nobody is allowed to ignore is a warning that gets
fixed.

We are not upstream. We are a downstream consumer pinning one commit of
acidanthera/audk and one release of OpenCorePkg, and compiling them with
whatever C compiler the host distribution happens to ship. We cannot fix
their warnings. Patching upstream source to satisfy a compiler upstream
never used would be us maintaining a fork we did not intend to have, and
it would grow by a hunk per compiler release, forever.

A NEW COMPILER INVENTS NEW WARNINGS. This is the second instance, not
the first, which is why this patch fixes the class rather than the case:

  GCC 15  defaults to -std=gnu23, under which OpenCorePkg's vendored
          libDER does not compile at all. That one is a real change of
          language, not a new diagnostic, and it is fixed properly by
          stating the dialect -- patch 0002 here, and the dialect
          internal/firmware passes OpenCorePkg. Not this.

  GCC 16  squirrel-zapper, 2026-09-20, gcc 16.2.1. The OpenCore build
          finished clean in 397 s; the OVMF build died in 26 s:

            MdeModulePkg/Library/CustomizedDisplayLib/CustomizedDisplayLib.c:435:18:
            error: variable 'Count' set but not used
            [-Werror=unused-but-set-variable=]
            cc1: all warnings being treated as errors

          Note the trailing `=` in the diagnostic's name: GCC 16 gave
          that warning a level argument, so it is not even spelled the
          way GCC 13 spells it.

`Count` is genuinely set and never read, in a file we do not own, did not
write and have no standing to change. -Wno-unused-but-set-variable would
make today's error go away and teach nothing, and GCC 17 will bring a
diagnostic nobody has heard of yet. The rule this patch states instead:
A WARNING WE CANNOT ACT ON SHOULD NOT STOP OUR BUILD. -Werror belongs to
whoever can fix the warning.

Upstream keeps its discipline -- nothing here changes what tianocore or
acidanthera see when they build these trees. We stop inheriting a build
failure from it.

THE WARNINGS ARE STILL PRINTED. -Wno-error cancels the promotion to
errors and nothing else: -Wall is still on, cc1 still says everything it
said before, and all of it is still in ovmf-build.log. Warnings becoming
invisible would be a regression and would mean this patch is the wrong
shape; warnings becoming non-fatal is the entire intent.

IT DOES NOT CHANGE THE BYTES, and that was measured rather than assumed,
because decisions/0004's whole claim is that this firmware is
reproducible. Cold builds either side of this patch on pet-power-plant,
gcc 13.3.0, 2026-09-19 -- all three images byte-identical:

  OVMF_CODE.fd  195c4dcff2abf2f5aea08c290f057432704a250eab56b0b887ac0f8503ee58d2
  OVMF_VARS.fd  5d2ac383371b408398accee7ec27c8c09ea5b74a0de0ceea6513388b15be5d1e
  OVMF.fd       e44f708330318e8963baea94c91ed265761794e565e6a48db8821e92e4cbda17

which is what -Werror ought to do: it decides whether a diagnostic aborts
the build, not what the compiler emits.

Applied by internal/firmware's OVMF build, guarded by a grep so a warm
tree is not patched twice and asserted afterwards so a patch that silently
did nothing cannot pass for success -- the shape 0001 and 0002 also use.
It is a separate patch from 0002 because it is a separate claim: 0002
says which language this is, this one says whose job it is to fix a
warning.

--- a/OvmfPkg/OvmfPkgX64.dsc
+++ b/OvmfPkg/OvmfPkgX64.dsc
@@ -119,5 +119,14 @@
   INTEL:*_*_*_CC_FLAGS = /D TDX_GUEST_SUPPORTED
   GCC:*_*_*_CC_FLAGS = -D TDX_GUEST_SUPPORTED
 
+  #
+  # -Werror is upstream's discipline for upstream's own development.
+  # We are a downstream consumer pinning a commit we cannot patch, so a
+  # warning we cannot act on must not stop our build. Still printed,
+  # just no longer fatal. See the patch header for the whole argument.
+  # packer-plugin-macosx: assets/firmware/patches/0003-firmware-drop-werror.patch
+  #
+  GCC:*_*_*_CC_FLAGS = -Wno-error
+
 !include NetworkPkg/NetworkBuildOptions.dsc.inc
 
